From: weewx-rtldavis (WeatheredScientist fork)
Subject: rtldavis: time-gate the duplicate-packet filter

The Go demodulator drops any packet whose 8 payload bytes equal the previous
one's, with no time bound.  That conflates two unrelated events:

  * the same RF burst decoded twice out of overlapping sample blocks, a few
    milliseconds apart -- a genuine duplicate, correctly dropped; and
  * the transmitter re-sending an unchanged payload one full loop period
    later (>= 2.5625 s even for id 0) -- a NEW transmission we actually
    received.

Dropping the second kind takes the `continue` before the hop handling runs, so
the pending loopTimer expires into the "packet missed" branch a few hundred ms
later and books a received packet as a loss.  On a live Davis 6263 / VP2+ ISS
that is ~27% of all transmissions: a 15-minute standalone capture recorded 295
hops, 214 decoded, 81 "packet missed" -- 80 of them preceded within 0.6 s by a
"duplicate packet" line.  Real loss was 1 in 295.  rxCheckPercent under-reported
a ~99% link as ~73% for the life of the receiver.

The two populations separate cleanly in time.  In that capture, re-decodes
clustered at ~2 ms (median 2.1 ms, max under 50 ms) and transmitter re-sends at
~2812 ms (= one loop period for transmitter id 4), with nothing at all in
between -- a 1300x gap.  Each re-send also carried its own distinct correlation
magnitude and symbol vector, on a different channel after a retune, so they were
demodulated from fresh samples rather than replayed from a stale buffer.

This patch gates the existing byte comparison on -dupwindow (default 500 ms),
comfortably above the re-decode cluster and below the shortest possible loop
period, so a real transmission can never be swallowed on any transmitter id.
Packets past the window take the normal path: counted, logged, and hopped on.

Applies to the rtldavis source bundled in src.tgz (VERSION 0.15).
Upstream: https://github.com/lheijst/rtldavis

